Today's Target
By the end of today, you should be able to confidently explain Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability, Authentication, Authorization, and Non-repudiation—and explain why they matter in a real product.

**What is Cybersecurity?**
Cybersecurity is protecting systems, data, devices and services from unauthorized access, misuse, modification, disruption or destruction.
An internet-connected HVAC controller may contain:
•	Configuration
•	Temperature data
•	Credentials
•	Network information
•	Firmware
•	Cloud credentials
An attacker could potentially:
•	Read configuration
•	Change temperature
•	Disable the controller
•	Install malicious firmware
•	Use the device to attack another system
5–15 min — CIA Triad
CIA means Confidentiality, Integrity and Availability. A useful mental model is: Who can see it? → Who can change it? → Can I use it when I need it?
1. Confidentiality
Only authorized people/systems should be able to access information.
HVAC example: An attacker intercepts communication and reads device ID, credentials or configuration. This is a confidentiality breach.
Common controls:
•	Encryption (TLS/AES)
•	Access control
•	Authentication
•	Network segmentation
•	Data classification
Interview answer: Confidentiality means preventing unauthorized disclosure or access to information. Encryption and access controls are common mechanisms used to protect confidentiality.
2. Integrity
Data or software should not be modified by an unauthorized party.
HVAC example: An attacker changes a temperature command from 24°C to 35°C.
Firmware example: An attacker replaces legitimate firmware with malicious firmware. If the device installs it without verifying the signature, integrity has failed.
Common controls:
•	Hashing/checksums
•	Digital signatures
•	Secure Boot
•	Firmware signing
•	Access control and audit logs
Interview answer: Integrity ensures that data or software has not been modified in an unauthorized or unintended manner. Digital signatures, hashes, access controls and secure boot can help maintain integrity.
3. Availability
Authorized users should be able to access a service when required.
Examples: DoS/DDoS, ransomware, server failure, network failure, resource exhaustion and power failure.
Common controls:
•	Redundancy
•	Failover
•	Backups
•	DDoS protection
•	Load balancing
•	Rate limiting
•	Disaster recovery
Easy CIA Example — ATM
Confidentiality: Only you should see your PIN, account balance and transaction information.
Integrity: Your ₹50,000 balance should not become ₹5,000 because someone modified the database.
Availability: The ATM should work when you need to withdraw money.
15–20 min — Authentication vs Authorization
Authentication = Who are you?
•	Username + password
•	MFA/OTP
•	Certificate
•	Biometric
•	Access token
Authorization = What are you allowed to do?
Example: An Engineer may view devices, view logs and change configuration, but not delete a customer or modify firmware.
Easy memory trick: Authentication = Identity. Authorization = Permission.
Non-repudiation
Non-repudiation means a party should not be able to credibly deny performing an action when reliable evidence ties the action to them.
•	Digital signatures
•	Certificates
•	Secure audit logs
•	Strong identity
Important: A username in a log by itself does not automatically provide strong non-repudiation.
20–23 min — Non-repudiation in Practice
A user digitally signs an important transaction. The signature can provide evidence that the transaction came from that user and was not altered.
23–28 min — Product Security Scenario
Architecture: Mobile App → Cloud API → IoT Device → Physical HVAC System
Attack #1: Attacker intercepts communication and reads device information. → Breaches Confidentiality.
Attack #2: Attacker changes a temperature command from 24°C to 40°C. → Breaches Integrity.
Attack #3: Attacker floods the cloud API and devices cannot connect. → Breaches Availability.
